sequenceDiagram
  %% Container runtime (ADR 0250 step 5, docs/reference/development-containers.md#refresh-after-a-release): after each Gateway release, the deployment of each nested-fleet default Instance to the release commit. It is `instance.deploy` with the fleet steps that only a nested-fleet default's intent runs. It refreshes the inner fleet and takes the seed that new Instances of that Project clone. For the nested-fleet path it replaces the release trigger and the daily run of docs/cli/task/vm-image-refresh.mmd, and it calls neither.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: none · after each Gateway release · instance:deploy on the default runs the same flow
  %% kind: instance.deploy
  autonumber
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  %% lane C icon=server pill=default tone=purple
  %% lane F icon=server pill=fleet tone=gray
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution
  participant N as app-dev Node<br/>reached over ssh
  participant C as Container<br/>default Instance
  participant F as Inner fleet<br/>3 containers
  Note over GW: Admit nested-fleet refresh<br/>release commit
  %%> Started by:
  %%> - the release step "Admit nested-fleet refresh", after the Gateway is verified and Horizon runs: `instances:refresh-nested-fleets --commit`
  %%> When:
  %%> - only while the Gateway setting `ORBIT_INSTANCES_NESTED_FLEET_REFRESH` is on; off, the step prints one line and admits nothing
  %%> - one deployment per nested-fleet default whose runtime is in `orbit-instances-nested` on a Node with the nested-fleet switch on
  %%> - none exists: the step prints one line and admits nothing
  %%> - a clone of the default is being created: refused, `instance.clone_in_flight`; the next release tries again
  %%> - a deployment of the default is open: refused by the Instance lock, `operation.target_busy`
  %%> - the release commit is missing from the Project's repository: the admission's source read refuses it, `project.source_unavailable`
  %%> Result:
  %%> - best effort: the step allows failure and nothing waits for it; the release stays live
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>instance-deploy-direct
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - the intent carries `fleet` (NestedFleetIntent): the fleet's names, the operator, the checkout path and the egress proxy
  %%> - the run budget grows by the fleet steps' bounds
  SW->>N: Start the container<br/>Ansible · tags start
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - the one start of a container; the Club key is delivered there, outside the container's disk
  SW->>F: Check the inner fleet<br/>Ansible · tags fleet_check
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - waits for the inner Incus, then compares the three nested containers with the fleet rules; it changes nothing
  %%> - refuses `instance.container_fleet_drift` when a rule Orbit does not write differs (`raw.idmap`), or a nested container or the inner bridge cannot be read; the Operation's result `fleet_drift` names each difference
  %%> - an owned rule that differs passes: the refresh writes it
  %%> Why:
  %%> - nothing changed yet, so a refusal restores no seed
  SW->>N: Keep the current seed<br/>Ansible · tags seed
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - retakes `seed-COMMIT` of the default as it is, fleet included, before the run changes anything
  %%> - takes the new snapshot first, as `next-COMMIT` beside the old `seed-COMMIT`; only then does the old one go and the new one take its name (docs/reference/development-containers.md#a-retake-keeps-the-seed)
  %%> - refuses `instance.fleet_key_on_disk` first when the Club key path is a file on the container's disk; the key is never read
  %%> On failure:
  %%> - a snapshot Incus cannot take, such as one with no free isolated ID range: `instance.container_seed_failed`, and the error detail names why; the old seed stays, nothing else changed, and the run fails with no restore
  %%> Why:
  %%> - a failed refresh then restores a seed that holds the fleet, never one from before it
  SW->>N: Fetch into the mirror<br/>Ansible · tags fetch
  SW->>C: Check out in the container<br/>Ansible · tags checkout
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - checks out the release commit in `/srv/workspace`; the nested Gateway and app-dev see it through their `checkout` device
  SW->>C: Run dev deploy steps<br/>incus exec · vault copy
  SW->>C: Install the Processes<br/>Ansible · tags processes
  SW->>F: Start the inner fleet<br/>Ansible · tags fleet_start
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - compares the fleet with its rules as the check does, starts the nested containers that are stopped, links the Club key, brings up `wg-nested` with `ip` and `wg`
  %%> - checks only what a start can promise: the three containers run and the tunnel reaches the hub; no Doctor wait
  %%> - changes no device or configuration key: on a running fleet it only reads
  %%> Why:
  %%> - the checkout just moved under the running nested Gateway; until the refresh migrates and restarts it, its Doctor cannot be healthy
  %%> - it takes the hub's key only when it is a WireGuard key: just after a boot, `wg` prints `(none)` with exit 0 until `wg-quick` has set the key
  %%> On failure:
  %%> - no key within 60 s: the step says the nested Gateway's WireGuard key was not ready, and sets no peer
  SW->>F: Refresh the inner fleet<br/>Ansible · tags fleet_refresh
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - reads the refresh record in the container (`/var/lib/orbit/fleet-refresh.json`) and the digest `code` of the checkout: `apps/gateway`, which holds the migrations and the Composer lock of packages that ship migrations, and `apps/agent/contract`, which it reads
  %%> - after the migrate, makes the digest `converge` from what a base converge reads: the `swamp` files that `swamp/ansible/fleet-converge-inputs.json` lists, the nested Gateway's digest of each Node's base intent (`orbit:node-converge-digest`, which admits and writes nothing) and the fleet intent
  %%> - a part runs when its digest differs from the record; a missing record, or one it cannot read, makes every part run, and an unreadable record adds a warning
  %%> - the converge also runs when a digest cannot be read, or when the last converge that ran (`converged` in the record) is more than 7 days old
  %%> - every run: waits until the state mounts of the nested Gateway (with its Swamp state when the Club key is there) and app-dev are active (60 s), places the agent delivery (into the checkout) and reloads the grants (on the nested Gateway's own disk)
  %%> - with the Club key, every run: reads the nested Gateway's Swamp tokens (`swamp access token list`, names and dates only); they are due when one of the six (serve driver, Gateway run and read, the access of workers a, b and c) is missing or older than 20 days (`fleet_token_max_age`)
  %%> - the tokens are due: the `remint` play stops the workers and serve, makes the worker set whole (folder and tokens of a missing worker), renews all six, and enables and starts serve and the three workers; the step ends only when serve and the three workers are active, so nothing else of the refresh runs on them meanwhile; a remint marks the nested Gateway for `base` and restarts its services, which read the new tokens
  %%> - `code` changed: migrates the nested Gateway and restarts its services
  %%> - every run: reads the fleet rules on the running fleet and writes the fleet files that differ
  %%> - the converge is due: waits until the three agents are online (300 s), converges the base of the three nested Nodes one by one, and reads how many tasks each changed; a failed converge fails the refresh
  %%> - every run: waits for readiness once (Doctor healthy, three agents, the operator), so the seed holds a ready fleet
  %%> - retakes `base` only for the containers whose disk changed: a step that writes a nested disk and reported a change (the migrate that applied one, the grants reload that changed a file), that container; a converge that changed a Node, that Node and the nested Gateway; a missing `base`, that container; a missing or unreadable record or a rule or file that differed, all three
  %%> - each of them stops first; the others keep running; with all three stopped it writes the owned rules (and a build rule only where it is absent), and it checks every rule; with some stopped the rules only compare, and a difference fails the refresh
  %%> - otherwise the fleet keeps running and `base` stays
  %%> - every run, after `base`: keeps the pinned shape tools in the Instance container at `/opt/orbit-shape-gate`, named by the checkout's own `bin/swamp-shape-gate` from its three pin files; when the venv and the collections of these pins are complete it only reads them; otherwise it installs `python3-venv` when it is missing, builds them and removes the tools of other pins
  %%> - removes the record before its first write to a nested disk and writes it again last, so a run that stops in the middle leaves none, and the next run does every part and retakes `base` of all three
  %%> - the result names each part, ran or skipped, why the converge ran or was skipped, the tasks the converges changed, what it did with the tokens and why (`tokens`, `tokens_reason`), what it did with the shape tools and why (`tools`, `tools_reason`), `base` retaken or kept and for which containers, and the seconds of each part
  %%> - every API call goes from the container over `wg-nested`, never through the container's egress proxy; a failed Doctor wait names the last status and body, or curl's error
  %%> Why:
  %%> - most releases change nothing the nested Nodes hold: stopping the fleet and copying `base` three times cost about 3 minutes, and three base converges that change nothing about 87 s
  %%> - a file list cannot follow the Gateway code that builds a base intent, so the nested Gateway hashes the intent itself; the `swamp` list has a cover test
  %%> - the nested Gateway's Swamp tokens live 30 days, and a fleet lives longer: the refresh keeps them young, so no copy stops with expired tokens; an older fleet with only workers a and b gets worker c first, because the remint renews all three
  %%> - the converges run one by one: every step of the converge workflow has a fixed behavior name, so a second run at once waits for the first one's Swamp lock and fails after 60 s
  %%> - a reset restores `base` of all three in seconds; taking `base` costs about 32 s per container, so only what changed is retaken
  %%> - the set stays consistent: the nested Gateway's database holds the schema and records each converge, so its `base` is retaken with any other
  %%> - a snapshot of a running container on a directory pool is a file copy, not a consistent state, so `base` is taken with the fleet stopped
  %%> - the proof runner in a copy needs the pinned Ansible controller, and the default is never rebuilt from a new image: the seed after this step gives every copy the tools of its own commit, so no copy builds them or needs `python3-venv`
  %%> On failure:
  %%> - a failed tools install does not fail the refresh: the result says `tools: failed` with the step in `tools_reason`, the log shows the end of its error output, and the seed is taken without ready tools (the venv's `.complete` is written last); a proof in such a copy builds the tools in its own cache, and when that fails its fleet preparation fails with the line that names the missing pinned folder
  SW->>N: Take the seed snapshot<br/>Ansible · tags seed
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - refuses `instance.fleet_key_on_disk` when the Club key path is a file on the container's disk; the key is never read
  %%> - stops the Processes, snapshots `seed-COMMIT` again even at the same commit, starts them, deletes the older seeds
  %%> - at the same commit the new snapshot is `next-COMMIT` until it exists; then the old `seed-COMMIT` goes and the new one takes its name: never `--reuse`, which deletes first
  %%> - a default with an inner volume also snapshots the volume under the same name with the Processes stopped; the pair is the seed, and the older pair goes only after both new halves exist
  %%> - right before the volume's half: `sync -f /srv/inner-pool` as root in the container, so the block snapshot holds what btrfs has not committed yet (it commits every 30 s); no freeze: the halves are separate snapshots, so a freeze adds nothing over the flush
  %%> - a failed half or flush deletes only the new halves and fails `instance.container_seed_failed`, `instance.seed_volume_flush_failed` or `instance.seed_volume_snapshot_failed` with the cause in the detail; the old seed stays
  %%> - the inner fleet runs during the snapshot when the refresh kept `base`: on the directory pool the inner pool lies on the container's disk, so the seed is one point in time of the whole fleet, as after a power cut; on an inner volume the two halves are taken one after the other, apart by the flush: seconds at most under load
  %%> - the seed holds the refresh record of the fleet it holds
  SW->>C: Check the container<br/>Ansible · tags check
  SW->>F: Start the refreshed fleet<br/>Ansible · tags fleet_resume
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - starts the nested containers the refresh stopped for `base`, and waits for readiness; a fleet that kept running only gets the readiness wait
  %%> - fails with `instance.container_fleet_resume_failed`; the new seed and `base` stay, nothing is restored, and the detail says so
  %%> Why:
  %%> - the default runs on the new commit, fleet included
  alt every step passes
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event
    Note over GW: Record the seed commit
    %%> Result:
    %%> - new Instances of the Project clone the new seed; existing clones keep the fleet they have
  else a step fails from the checkout on
    SW->>N: Restore the old seed<br/>Ansible · tags fleet_restore
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - stops the container and restores its newest seed by creation time: the one "Keep the current seed" took, or a newer one
    %%> - a default with an inner volume restores the volume to its snapshot of the same name too; a failed volume restore fails the run with the container already restored
    %%> - refuses `instance.fleet_seed_missing` when no seed is left, or when the inner volume lacks the seed's half; both are checked before anything is restored
    SW->>N: Start on the old seed<br/>Ansible · tags start
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - starts it through `start`, so the start delivers what every start delivers
    SW->>F: Start the restored fleet<br/>Ansible · tags fleet_resume
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - starts the inner fleet of the restored seed and waits for readiness, so the default is as it was before the run
    %%> - on failure the error detail adds: the inner fleet of the restored seed did not start, and why
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>failed step
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the Operation names the first failed step and its code, such as `instance.container_fleet_refresh_failed`
    %%> - a fleet phase's error detail names the failed task and its message, such as the Doctor body
    %%> - the old seed stays the seed; clones keep copying it, and the next release runs again
    %%> - the release has ended: the Activity row `instance.refresh_failed` (status failed, command `instances:refresh-nested-fleets`) names the Operation, its code and its detail, so `activity:list` and the problem collector show it
  end
